==Research / Planning Answers==

(Q1) "I don't understand the architectural boundary between an Android system
     service and what's done by any given TTS engine implementation."

Short version: there is no Android *system* service that actually speaks. Android
ships (a) a contract — the abstract `TextToSpeechService` — and (b) a client API
— `android.speech.tts.TextToSpeech`. Your app binds (over Binder) to an
*independently-installed engine app* that implements that contract, and it's that
engine app where both the synthesis and the AudioTrack that plays the sound live.
On this phone the engine is com.brahmadeo.supertonic.tts (uid 10416); Google's is
com.google.android.tts. "The system service" only brokers the connection; the real
playback is third-party app code running in the engine's own process. That's
exactly why the audio player showed up under the engine's uid, not Wcast's.

Why doesn't the framework give TTS full media controls (stop exists, but not
pause, and no skip fwd/back)? Because the TTS API was designed for short,
"say this now" use (TalkBack/accessibility, assistant replies) — not long-form
playback. Its control surface is speak()/stop() plus QUEUE modes only. The
*media* controls — pause/seek/skip, media-button routing, the media notification
— belong to a separate subsystem: MediaSession + "real" audio players. Android
routes headset buttons only to the app whose *own uid* has an active
AudioTrack/MediaPlayer AND a matching active MediaSession. TTS engines never
register a MediaSession (they're not meant to be your media player), so they can
never be the button target. The two subsystems were never bridged in the
framework; bridging them is exactly what a reading app must do, and every
podcast/audiobook app does it the same way.

And "maybe that would be up to the implementers of the service?" — yes, pause/
stop are implemented by the engine's own code. But even a well-behaved engine that
pauses still pauses its *own uid's* AudioTrack; that never helps routing, because
routing keys off (uid + MediaSession), not "who paused".

(Q2) Is rendering TTS to WAV files the architecturally correct / Google-
     recommended approach? Any downsides?

Yes — this is the scenario `TextToSpeech.synthesizeToFile(...)` exists for:
"produce the audio, but let MY app own playback." It's the documented path for
any use the engine's built-in player can't serve: seek, save/replay, DSP, or
(our case) being a real MediaSession-backed player so hardware buttons route to
us. So it's a well-trodden, intended pattern, not a hack. (Note: synthesizeToFile
writes a WAV — RIFF header + PCM — by default, and both MediaPlayer and AudioTrack
play WAV/PCM natively, hence "WAV files".)

Honest review of downsides:
- Lead-in latency: synthesizeToFile must finish a whole utterance before you can
  start playing it, vs today's streaming speak(). On a real-time engine this is
  small; we can hide most of it by rendering a short lookahead (synthesize the
  next utterance while the current one plays).
- Transient disk: WAVs in cacheDir, delete-as-you-go. 16-bit 44.1k mono ≈ 5 MB
  per minute, so a long article = a few MB. Negligible on a phone.
- We re-own queue chaining + pause-at-boundary + error handling — which is the
  exact code a media library gives you for free (see Q3).
- The audio is "baked" at the chosen voice/rate/pitch at synthesize time;
  changing them mid-stream means re-synthesizing. We effectively re-speak on
  those changes today, so no regression.

(Q3) If we're moving to files, why not go straight to the "cleaner long-term"
     Media3 ExoPlayer? Any advantage to Option 1 over Option 2 besides fewer LOC?
     Which has greater odds of success?

Key framing: Option 2 is NOT an alternative to synthesizeToFile — it's Option 1
PLUS a media-player library. ExoPlayer plays files/URIs, not the TTS engine's
live stream, so either way we must first render text -> audio files. The real
choice is "wire our own MediaPlayer/AudioTrack" (Option 1) vs "adopt Media3
ExoPlayer" (Option 2).

Option 1 advantages (beyond fewer LOC):
- No new dependency (smaller APK, nothing new to learn/version).
- Smallest possible diff from today's SpeechController → fastest to a build we
  can test on the write phone, and cheapest to abandon if a device still misroutes.
- The AudioTrack variant can stream PCM straight out of the synthesizeToFile
  result with minimal buffering.

Option 2 advantages:
- Purpose-built for "a sequence of audio items with real pause/seek/skip":
  utterance chaining, gapless-ish joins, state machine, and lifecycle all free.
- Media3 MediaSessionService produces the media notification, lock-screen,
  drawer, and media-button wiring in the exact shape AntennaPod/Spotify use, so
  OEM routing quirks are least likely to bite.
- Future features (playback speed, skip-by-paragraph as a true seek) near-free.

Odds of success: for the specific "will the button route to Wcast" question the
two are EQUAL — the system only cares that Wcast's uid owns an active
AudioTrack/MediaPlayer plus a matching active MediaSession; it does not care
which player object we hold. So the outcome is decided by our session/flags, not
by the player choice. Option 2 wins on long-term robustness and less hand-rolled
state; Option 1 wins on least-effort-to-confirm.

